lesson physics-hot-and-cold v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=2 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Hot and cold"
  goal "Read a temperature off a thermometer, say how much warmer one reading is than another, and say which wrap keeps a drink warm."

  look {
    scene 23x24 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=start {
        row tubes space=3 align=start {
          thermometer hot from=0 to=40 step=10 value=32 unit="C"
          thermometer cold from=-10 to=30 step=10 value=2 unit="C"
        }
        daysky sky clouds=3 stars=0
      }
    }
    say """
      A thermometer tells you how hot something is. The column climbs when it warms up and drops
      when it cools down. The numbers are degrees. A hot drink cools down as its heat escapes, and
      wrapping it up slows that down.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.temp-read count=2 seed=6
    practice physics.warmer-of-two count=2 seed=3
    practice physics.temp-change count=2 seed=5
    practice physics.cup-warmest count=2 seed=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.cup-predict x="none" y="wool" hard-x="bubble"
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.cup-lost x="paper" m=20 easy-x="none" easy-m=10 hard-x="bubble"
  }
  remember {
    say "The taller the column, the warmer it is. A good wrap keeps the heat in."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read a real thermometer together, inside and then outside, and write both numbers down. If
    you can, fill two mugs with warm water, wrap one in a scarf, and feel both after twenty minutes.
    The prediction question asks which cup will stay warmer before the thermometers are shown; the
    last one needs two readings and a subtraction, which is a lot to hold at six, so do it on paper.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the thermometers read on the numbered marks, the rooms differ by twenty degrees or more, and the cups compared are bare and wool."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the readings fall on the half marks, the rooms differ by ten degrees, and the cups compared are bubble wrap and wool, which are close."
  }
}
